**Parallel runs in TestNG.** Set it in `testng.xml`:

```xml
<suite name="S" parallel="methods" thread-count="4">
```

`parallel` can be `methods`, `classes`, `tests` or `instances`. With the `@Test` annotation you can also use `@Test(threadPoolSize = 3, invocationCount = 6)`, and for data-driven tests `@DataProvider(parallel = true)`. Parallel runs need **thread-safe tests**: each thread needs its own `WebDriver` (store it in a `ThreadLocal<WebDriver>`), no shared mutable static state, and independent test data. Run it from Maven Surefire or on Selenium Grid.

**Causes of HTTP 500.** The server hit an unexpected condition: unhandled exception or null reference in code, database connection failure or query error, timeout calling a downstream service, misconfiguration or missing environment variable, resource exhaustion (memory, threads), deployment of a bad build, invalid data the server did not validate. To investigate: check server logs and stack trace, correlate with the request id, reproduce with the same payload, and compare against 4xx (client errors).

**Selenium snippets.**

```java
driver.get(url);
driver.findElement(By.id("user")).sendKeys("u");
driver.findElement(By.id("pass")).sendKeys("p");
driver.findElement(By.cssSelector("button[type=submit]")).click();

new Actions(driver).contextClick(el).perform();          // right click
new Select(driver.findElement(By.id("country"))).selectByVisibleText("India");

File f = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
Files.copy(f.toPath(), Path.of("shot.png"));
```

Use `WebDriverWait` with `ExpectedConditions` instead of `Thread.sleep`.

**Test scenarios for a pen.** Functional (writes smoothly, ink colour, cap or click mechanism), boundary (writing on different paper, angles, temperature), usability (grip, weight), durability (drop, ink lifetime), safety (non-toxic), and negative cases (leaks in a pocket, dried ink).

**WhatsApp.** Send and receive text, media and voice messages, group chats, delivery and read receipts, offline and poor network, notifications, end-to-end encryption, backup and restore, multi-device, permissions, localisation, performance and security. Split into functional, non-functional, and negative areas.
